# 5. Static vs Dynamic Analysis

This is one of the most important concepts in malware analysis.

## 5.1 Static Analysis

Static analysis examines a sample **without intentionally executing it**.

Common tools:

```text
PEStudio
CAPA
FLOSS
CFF Explorer
HxD
Ghidra
oledump
```

Questions:

```text
What type of file is this?
What are its hashes?
What strings are present?
What imports are present?
What capabilities are indicated?
What is the PE structure?
Are macros present?
What code or functions exist?
```

---

# 5.2 Dynamic Analysis

Dynamic analysis observes the sample while it executes in a controlled environment.

Common tools:

```text
Procmon
Process Explorer
Wireshark
INetSim
x64dbg
```

Questions:

```text
What processes are created?
What files are modified?
What registry keys are changed?
What network connections occur?
What DNS requests occur?
What remote endpoints are contacted?
What happens at runtime?
```

---

# 5.3 Why Use Both?

Static analysis can reveal what the program **contains or appears capable of doing**.

Dynamic analysis can reveal what the program **actually does during a particular execution**.

Neither method alone necessarily gives the complete picture.

```text
Static Evidence
      +
Dynamic Evidence
      +
Network Evidence
      +
Memory / Code Evidence
      |
      v
Correlated Analysis
```

---

