# 9. Important Investigation Principle — Correlation

One of the most important lessons in malware analysis is:

> **Do not make a final conclusion from a single indicator.**

Example:

```text
Suspicious Import
       |
       v
Interesting String
       |
       v
CAPA Capability
       |
       v
Runtime Process Activity
       |
       v
Network Connection
       |
       v
Memory / Code Evidence
       |
       v
Correlated Conclusion
```

Each observation adds context.

For example:

```text
Creates Process
```

alone is not necessarily malicious.

But if the investigation also finds:

```text
Creates Process
+
Suspicious PowerShell Command
+
Downloads File
+
Connects to Unusual Remote Endpoint
+
Creates Persistence
```

the combined evidence provides much stronger grounds for further investigation.

